{-
   An owned container reaches a `[T]` parameter as a window over its own buffer, and the elements
   have to be the window's - Implementation-Containers.md §6.

   The conversion is a descriptor rather than a copy: `{base, length}` built over the array's own
   run, so an `Array(Int)` at an `[I64]` parameter reads the same bytes at twice the width - two
   elements as one, then a load past the end of the run. Under `&` it is that descriptor with a
   store through it.

   convertSlice is the only place that asks. A non-generic parameter written `[I64]` folds to
   `Flat(I64)` and its call reaches convert() directly, since a concrete signature never goes
   through the solver - so nothing between the argument and the descriptor compares anything, and
   the growable owner was exempt here on the grounds that `Array(T)` and `Flat(U)` are already
   different types. That is the reason a conversion is attempted, not a reason one cannot be built.
-}

fn narrow(xs: [Int]) -> Int = 0
fn wide(xs: [I64]) -> Int = 0
fn wideMut(&xs: [I64]) -> {} = {}
fn bytes(xs: [U8]) -> Int = 0

-- Refused: the array holds `Int` and the window says `I64`. The two spellings look like a width the
-- compiler widens for free, and a window widens across nothing at all.
fn valuePosition() -> Int:
    let xs = [7, 8] :: [Int]
    return wide(xs)

-- The same refusal with the sigil on it, which is the half that used to write through the
-- descriptor. borrowArgument commits to the conversion as soon as it sees an owner against a
-- window, so the message has to come from there - without it this was reported as an argument that
-- was not there at all.
fn mutablePosition() -> {}:
    let &xs = [7, 8] :: [Int]
    wideMut(xs)

-- Narrower rather than wider, which read one byte of the four and is refused in the same sentence:
-- the question is whether the elements are the same type, not whether one fits the other.
fn narrowerElement() -> Int:
    let xs = [7, 8] :: [Int]
    return bytes(xs)

-- A `[T *n]`, whose elements were compared all along, and an `@inline(n) @capacity(n)` array, whose
-- were too. Here so that one fixture asserts all three owners rather than whichever one happened to
-- have a test of its own.
fn fixedOwner() -> Int:
    let xs = [7, 8] :: [Int *2]
    return wide(xs)

fn refinedOwner() -> Int:
    let &xs = [7, 8] :: @inline(2) @capacity(2) [Int]
    return wide(xs)

-- Not this: the elements agree, so each of the three owners becomes a window and nothing here is
-- reported.
fn accepted() -> Int:
    let &grow = [1, 2, 3] :: [Int]
    let fixed = [4, 5] :: [Int *2]
    let &refined = [6, 7] :: @inline(2) @capacity(2) [Int]
    return narrow(grow) + narrow(fixed) + narrow(refined)

fn main() -> Int = 0
